        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
Human spaceflight is associated with a loss of body protein. Previous studies of nitrogen balance during spaceflight have reported a persistent negative nitrogen balance. Specific changes include a loss of lean body mass, decreased muscle mass in the calves, and decreased muscle strength. The main objective of this experiment was to determine the changes in human protein metabolism associated with spaceflight. To achieve this objective, whole body protein synthesis, breakdown and plasma protein synthesis rates were measured before, during and after the flight by the 15N glycine method. In addition, nitrogen intake and excretion were monitored continuously from launch minus seven days through landing plus six days for the determination of nitrogen balance and the urinary 3-methyl histidine excretion rate.
<P>
APPROACH:<BR>
Data were collected in the following sessions:<P>

<u>Preflight</u><P>

Protein metabolism study, Height measurement, Body mass measurement, Urinary data (creatinine, cortisol, C peptide, interleukin 6), Urinary data (3 methyl histidine, hydroxylysine, hydroxyproline)
<P>
<u>Inflight</u><P>

Protein metabolism study, Body mass measurement, Urinary data (creatinine, cortisol, C peptide, interleukin 6), Urinary data (3 methyl histidine, hydroxylysine, hydroxyproline), Urine monitoring system calibration, Body mass measurement device calibration
<P>
<u>Postflight</u><P>

Protein metabolism study, Body mass measurement, Urinary data (creatinine, cortisol, C peptide, interleukin 6), Urinary data (3 methyl histidine, hydroxylysine, hydroxyproline)
<P>
RESULTS:<BR>
Nitrogen balance was decreased during spaceflight. The decrease in nitrogen balance was greatest 
on the first day when food intake was reduced and again toward the end of the mission. An 
approximately 30% increase in protein synthesis above the preflight baseline was found for flight 
day 8 for all 6 subjects (p<0.05), indicating that the astronauts showed a stress response to 
spaceflight.<p>
        </div>

